 0.017608642578125, 0.040069580078125, 0.00295257568359375, 0.00911712646484375, 0.016448974609375, 0.0240478515625, -0.0136871337890625, -0.004650115966796875, -0.03594970703125, -0.00994110107421875, -0.0208282470703125, 0.01195526123046875, 0.01178741455078125, -0.01227569580078125, -0.01239013671875, 0.0014104843139648438, 0.005046844482421875, 2.664327621459961e-05, -0.0200042724609375, 0.01227569580078125, 0.038055419921875, -0.033782958984375, -0.0245819091796875, -0.00933837890625, -0.0035858154296875, -0.011810302734375, 0.0131988525390625, 0.005977630615234375, -0.0026416778564453125, 0.0167388916015625, -0.025299072265625, -0.0223236083984375, -0.0113067626953125, -0.00673675537109375, 0.0218505859375, -0.00870513916015625, 0.0233306884765625, -0.039306640625, 0.0019989013671875, -0.0272216796875, -0.018890380859375, -0.0085601806640625, 0.00982666015625, -0.0109100341796875, -0.01364898681640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Permanental processes are a class of stochastic point process models that have been used in spatial statistics and machine learning, particularly for modeling repulsion between points (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006). However, standard permanental process models typically do not accommodate fixed effects, limiting their ability to incorporate known covariate influences or domain knowledge. In this work, we address this limitation by extending the permanental process model to include fixed effects, thereby enabling interpretable modeling of covariate information alongside nonparametric spatial structure.\n\nOur main contribution is to formulate a permanental process model for a point process on a domain \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) with an intensity function of the form\n\\[\n\\lambda(x) = \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\( z(x) \\) is a vector of known covariates at location \\( x \\), \\( \\beta \\) is a vector of fixed effect coefficients, and \\( f \\) is a function in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) associated with a positive definite kernel \\( k \\). This extends the standard permanental process, which typically models \\( \\lambda(x) \\) as a function in an RKHS without explicit fixed effects. By including \\( \\beta^\\top z(x) \\), our model allows for direct incorporation of interpretable covariate effects, distinguishing it from prior work such as McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006), where such fixed effects are not present.\n\nWe place a Gaussian prior on \\( f \\) and a Gaussian prior with large variance (i.e., a diffuse or non-informative prior) on \\( \\beta \\). In the limit as the prior variance on \\( \\beta \\) tends to infinity, the prior becomes non-informative, allowing the data to determine the fixed effects. However, this diffuse prior limit may lead to identifiability or overfitting issues, especially in small-sample settings, and regularization of the fixed effects may be necessary in practice.\n\nFor inference, we use penalized likelihood estimation. The log-likelihood for observed points \\( \\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\) is\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ \\beta^\\top z(x_i) + f(x_i) \\right] - \\int_{\\mathcal{X}} \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right) dx.\n\\]\nWe maximize the penalized log-likelihood\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) - \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|^2_{\\mathcal{H}_k} - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) is the RKHS norm and \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is the prior variance for \\( \\beta \\). In the diffuse prior limit (\\( \\sigma^2 \\to \\infty \\)), the penalty on \\( \\beta \\) vanishes. The representer theorem ensures that the solution for \\( f \\) lies in the span of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points. Thus, the estimated intensity function decomposes into a sum of a fixed effects term and an RKHS function, with the RKHS norm controlling the complexity of the nonparametric component.\n\nThis decomposition facilitates scientific interpretation: the fixed effects \\( \\beta \\) are directly related to interpretable covariates, while the RKHS component \\( f \\) captures residual structure. For example, in ecological applications, environmental variables can be included as fixed effects, while spatial dependence is modeled via the RKHS component. However, the model assumes that covariates are correctly specified and that the kernel is appropriate for the data. If these assumptions are violated, model performance may suffer. Guidance on covariate and kernel selection can be drawn from domain knowledge, cross-validation, or model diagnostics such as residual analysis.\n\nOur approach is related to semiparametric models in the literature (see Wahba, 1990), but differs from standard permanental process models by explicitly incorporating fixed effects. For further mathematical details and proofs, see McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006) and Wahba (1990).\n\nIn summary, we extend the permanental process model to include fixed effects with a diffuse prior, providing a flexible framework that balances interpretability and nonparametric modeling. This enables straightforward incorporation of domain knowledge and offers a principled way to model both covariate effects and residual structure. Future work should include empirical evaluation and further investigation of regularization strategies for the fixed effects."}}